By taking advantages of the nanosize, potentials of MOFs are broadened by nanoMOFs,[1] including application in biomedical sensing and imaging,[2] functional membranes,[3] thin film devices,[4] cancer therapy[5] and drug delivery.[6] The size of nanomaterials is critical for their properties and utility. A small change of size within nanoscale (say, tens of nanometers) may have a decisive impact on some properties of nanomaterials.[7] One of the widely studied examples is the pivotal role of size in the biological properties of nanoparticles such as cellular internalization and biodistribution.[8] Although great advances have been witnessed in synthesis and utilization of new MOFs over the past two decades, the understanding of size—property relationship of MOFs is still in infancy due to the difficulty of controlling size for many MOFs. Size effect has not been considered in most literature and the influence of size on many properties of nanoMOFs remains obscure.
To modulate a known sized MOF to another specific size by previous reported approaches such as modulated synthesis[9], microwave assisted synthesis,[10] or microemulsion strategy, [2a,11] a trial-and-error process is needed to optimize the reaction conditions (such as solvent, additive, temperature and so on), which is time and cost consuming and inefficient. Furthermore, the change of reaction conditions or the addition of additives may influence the major properties of the products (such as crystallinity, porosity and stability). In some cases, the range of size control is limited due to the narrow window of reaction conditions for producing the products with acceptable performance. In addition, since the microemulsion strategy requires a multicomponent system to form microemulsion droplets (generally consist of water/oil/surfactant), the application scope of this approach is limited by the poor solubility of some ligands and the poor water stability of some MOFs. [11a]
Based on the classical crystallization mechanism, the key to synthesize uniform nanocrystals is the separation of nucleation and growth.[12] Due to the energy barrier of homogeneous nucleation (Figure S1, Supporting Information), the critical supersaturation degree for nucleation $(S_{n})$ is higher than that for growth $(S_{g})$ . By maintaining the supersaturation degree during the growth process below $S_{n}$ and higher than $\scriptstyle{S_{g}}$ , simultaneous nucleation during growth can be avoided. However, it is difficult to achieve this ideal condition, especially in the reactions that all reactants are directly mixed and reacted under a hash reaction condition in the synthesis of most nanoMOFs.
To control the supersaturation degree of reactions, here we use syringe pump to separately deliver metal and ligand stock solutions at a controlled feed rate into a reaction system under stirring (Figure 1a, see photograph of a typical reactor in Figure S2). With reactants continuously supplied and consumed, there is a balance between the supply and consumption of monomers in this approach. The whole reaction process can be explained by the famous LaMer diagram (Figure 1b) in LaMer’s model.[13]
At the beginning of reaction, the supersaturation degree increases very quickly as monomers are continuously delivered into the reaction system (stage I). Nucleation begins once $S_{n}$ is excessed. Both the formation of nuclei and growth of formed nuclei will increase the consumption rate of monomers. As a result, the supersaturation degree is reduced to a level below $S_{n}$ very quickly. Therefore, nucleation is finished very fast, the same as the “burst nucleation” in LaMer’s model (stage II). After that, monomers are continuously consumed by the growth of nanoMOFs and supplied in controlled rate to maintain a low supersaturation degree below $S_{n}$ (Figure 1c), and thus a controlled growth process without nucleation (stage III) is ensured. By finishing the feeding of reactant at any time during stage III, supersaturation degree drops below $S_{g,}$ and the growth of nanoparticles can be stopped.
Firstly, we focus on the size controlled synthesis of an archetypical compound HKUST-1.[14] Based on the basic assumption, nucleation is assumed to occur only at the beginning of reaction in this approach. Therefore, we firstly studied the influence of feed rate at the beginning of reaction on the products. In three parallel reaction batches, $9.5~\mathrm{\mL}$ $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{O A c})_{2}{\cdot}\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ solution (ethanol/DMF= 1/1, $15~\mathsf{m M}$ ) and $9.5~\mathrm{mL}$ btc solution (DMF, $10~\mathsf{m M}$ , btc $\mathrel{\mathop:}=1,3,5$ -benzenetricarboxylate) were delivered into a three-neck bottle containing 1 mL DMF/ethano $\scriptstyle|=3/1$ solution at $45^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ (here after, the solution in the reaction bottle before the addition of monomers is called ‘initial solution’) through two separate channels of a syringe pump with feed rates of 114 mL/h, 38 mL/h and $12.7~\mathrm{mL/h}$ , respectively. After that, more monomer stock solution was added into the reaction bottles with the reaction parameters consistent in all three reaction bottles. To monitor the reactions, certain amounts of reaction solutions were collected at different reaction stages after the addition of different amounts of reactants (6 kinds of products were obtained in each reaction batch). As confirmed by field-emission scanning electron microscopy (FESEM) (see representative FESEM images in Figure 2a-c, and FESEM images of all the obtained 18 kinds of products in Figure S3), all the products are uniform nanoparticles. Power X-ray diffraction (PXRD) of differently sized nanoparticles demonstrates that the products are crystalline HKUST-1 (Figure 2d). As shown in Figure 2e, the size of nanoHKUST-1 increases with the addition of reactants. In addition, the size of the product with the addition of same amount of reactants is reduced when the feed rate at the beginning of reaction is increased.
As shown in Figure 2f, there is a linear relationship between the amount of added reactants and the third power of the average diameter of nanoHKUST-1 ( $d^{\beta}$ , which is proportional to the volume of the obtained three-dimensional nanoHKUST-1).
During the process of crystallization, the amount of reacted monomers is proportional to the volume of formed crystals. Therefore, to ensure the linear relationship as mentioned above, two requirements should be satisfied. Firstly, the number of the growing nanoparticles should remain constant. The formation of new nuclei will lead to heterogeneous size and smaller average diameter thus causing the deviation from this linear relationship. Secondly, the supersaturation degree (free monomer concentration) during this process should remain unchanged, thus the amount of reacted monomers can be proportional to the amount of the added reactants. Therefore, the ${\mathcal{I}}^{\beta}.$ —amount of reactants linear dependence is the key evidence for the successful separation of nucleation and growth by controlling the supersaturation degree. More importantly, the amount of reactants needed for the synthesis of nanoMOFs of particular sizes within a wide range can be calculated through the $d^{\beta}-$ amount of added reactants linear dependence. Therefore, the the growth of nanoMOFs is precisely predicable.
With the successful separation of nucleation and growth, we can explain why increasing feed rate at the beginning of reaction can reduce the size of products with the addition of same amount of reactants. An increased feed rate during nucleation increases the number of formed nuclei required to reduce supersaturation to a degree below $S_{n}$ and thus decreases the average size of products. By changing feed rate at the beginning of reaction, a “controlled nucleation” can be achieved.
The influence of feed rate during the “controlled growth” on the products was also studied and results show that increasing the feed rate to a high value during the growth process may lead to a “secondary nucleation”, and result in the products with bimodal sizes. The size of products remains unchanged when the feed rate changes below the maximum feed rate (Figure S4). This result further verifies that controlling reaction system at a low supersaturation degree by keeping a balance between the consumption and supply of monomers is the key for synthesizing uniform nanoparticles in this approach. Changing the concentration of the monomer stock solutions will not affect the separation of nucleation and growth. We have synthesized nanoHKUST-1 by monomer stock solution of other concentrations with the same successful separation of nucleation and growth, as confirmed by the $d^{3}.$ —amount of added reactants linear dependence (Figures S5-S6). The change of other reaction conditions such as temperature or solvents will affect the nucleation process, growth process as well as the quality (such as crystallinity and porosity) of products. Therefore, the optimization of these reaction conditions is needed to ensure the quality of the products. However, if dispersed crystalline nanoparticles can be synthesis under a certain reaction condition, the modulation of the size of nanoMOFs can be achieved easily through the nucleation control and growth control, and neither addition of additives nor modulation of reaction conditions by trial-and-error process included in previously reported approaches is needed. For nanoHKUST-1, changing the reaction temperature to $30~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ or $60~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , crystalline nanoHKUST-1 of different sizes can also be synthesis by our approach (Figures S7-S8).
Since nucleation occurs at the beginning of reactions, minor regulations on initial solution may have a decisive impact on nucleation. However, the influence of regulation of initial solution on the growth process is gradually weakened with the continuous addition of monomers solution. With this in mind, a convenient method for nucleation control has been developed by using different initial solutions containing a small amount of metal or ligand, which will significantly change the metal/ligand ratio during nucleation and will be diluted as monomers solution continuously added into the reaction system. As confirmed by the uniform size of nanoparticles in FEMSEM images (Figures S9-S10) and the ${\mathcal{I}}^{\beta}.$ —amount of reactants linear dependence (Figure S11), both the “controlled nucleation” and the “controlled growth” are achieved in these reactions. The excess of metal or ligand during nucleation will lead to the formation of more nuclei, thus decreasing the average size of nanoparticles obtained with the addition of same amount of reactants. PXRD results demonstrate that a small amount of ligand or metal in the initial solution will not affect the crystallinity of the products (Figure S12). Since FESEM images shown in each column in Figure 3 are of nanoHKUST-1 obtained from the same reaction batch, the difference of size results from the “controlled growth” that controlled by the amount of reactants. FESEM images in each row are of nanoHKUST-1 synthesized in parallel reaction batches with the addition of same amount of reactants. Thus the difference of size results from the different nuclei numbers in different reaction batches (nucleation control). As demonstrated above, with the independently controlled nucleation and growth in this strategy, size modulation is more feasible, which can easily meet the requirements for the synthesis of nanoMOFs with required sizes in required scales.
With the control over nucleation and growth separatedly,   
reaction conditions for nucleation and growth can be adjusted   
independently and other modulations in nucleation such as   
additives, temperature, concentration can also be flexibly   
employed. Furthermore, since the growth process in this   
approach is a seed mediated growth,[15] theoretically, using a   
small-sized nanoMOFs as seeds, nanoMOFs of any size that   
larger than the seeds can be synthesized. Although synthetic   
approaches involve the continuous addition of precursor have   
previously been employed for the synthesis of monodisperse   
inorganic nanocrystals such as metal oxides, sulfides and   
phosphides, [16] similar approaches have not yet been used in   
synthesis of MOFs. Moreover, the convenient nucleation control method developed in this work has not been reported.
To test the versatility of our strategy, we extended this approach to the synthesis of 8 other types of MOF, which are  widely studied due to their great potentials in real-life applications, including MIL101(Fe),[17] MIL-101-NH2(Fe),[18] MIL-100(Fe),[19] ZIF8,[20] MOF-801,[21] ZIF-90,[22] ZIF-67,[23] and UIO$66(Z r)^{[24]}$ (Figure 4). Figure 4ii-iv are representative FESEM images of differently sized nanoMOFs (see FESEM images of all the obtained nanoMOFs and other evidences for nucleation and growth control in Figures S13-S29). In all cases, both nucleation and growth control can be achieved as confirmed by the uniform size and the ${\mathcal{I}}^{\beta}.$ —amount of reactants linear dependence. For each nanoMOF, nanoparticles of different sizes (at least 6 different sizes) ranging from few tens to hundreds nanometers were obtained. The PXRD patterns of nanoMOFs of different sizes match well with the simulated patterns based on the targeted crystal structure without any extra peaks, illustrating the phase purity of all the nanoMOFs(Figure 4v). The uniformity of products was further confirmed by dynamic light scattering (DLS) experiments. Most colloidal solutions containing nanoMOFs have a polydispersity index (PDI) lower than 0.1, and a few samples have PDI values larger than 0.1 but lower than 0.15 (Figures S30-S38). The high surface areas in ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption experiments confirm that nanoMOFs synthesized by this approach maintain their high porosity nature (Figures S39-S47, summarized in Table S1).
Scale-up production of nanoMOFs is challenging but critical for their implementation in real-life applications. To demonstrate the applicability of this approach in large-scale synthesis, MIL101(Fe) and UIO-66(Zr), two of the most widely studied MOFs,
