were synthesized at a 5 L scale reaction. The syringe pump was replaced by a peristaltic pump to continuously deliver the monomers in the 5L scale synthesis (Figure S48). For MIL101(Fe), nanoparticles of the size (quantity) of $88~\mathsf{n m}$ (5.6 g), $143\ \mathsf{n m}$ (12.9 g) and $238~\mathsf{n m}$ (17.9 g) were obtained within the reaction times of 40 minutes, 2 hours and 3.75 hours, respectively. Likewise, nanoUIO-66(Zr) of the size (quantity) of $49\ \mathsf{n m}$ $(10.0\ g)$ , 88 nm (13.7 g), and $168\ \mathsf{n m}$ (18.1 g) were obtained within 50 minutes, 100 minutes and 160 minutes, respectively. All the products are uniform nanoparticles with high crystallinities and high surface areas as confirmed by PXRD, FESEM, DLS and ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption experiments (Figures S49-S55).
Unlike previous approaches for nanoMOFs synthesis that involve hash reaction conditions such as microwave, high temperature hydro(solvo)thermal or need the addition of additives, all the reactions in this work were carried out below the boiling point of solvents under atmospheric pressure at stirring without the addition of any additives. For nanoMIL101(Fe) and nanoMIL-101-NH2(Fe), unprecedented mild reaction conditions are reported here, i.e., at moderate temperature $(50^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ in $D M F/H_{2}O$ under air atmospheric pressure without the addition of any corrosive additive. The mild reaction conditions can greatly reduce the apparatus and operation cost and alleviate safety concerns in larger scale synthesis.[25] Besides, the peristaltic pump used in this approach is conventional equipment, which has been widely used in various
